00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:12.800
Common sense would say that you and I and everyone else will agree on what's happening, or what exists, right now, moment after moment after moment.

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:18.770
That is, we would all agree on what lies in a given now slice.

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:28.500
But Einstein showed that, strangely, when you take motion into account, this common sense picture of time goes out the window.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:32.040
Do you see what I mean?

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:35.690
Think of space-time as a loaf of bread.

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:41.740
Einstein realized that just as there are different ways to cut a loaf of bread into individual

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:48.420
slices, there are different ways to cut space-time into individual now slices.

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:55.200
That is, because motion affects the passage of time, someone who's moving will have a different

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:58.180
conception of what's happening right now.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:23:59.970
And so they'll cut the...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.140
loaf into different now slices.

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.780
Their slices will be at a different angle.

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:09.340
That person who's moving will tilt the knife,

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.640
will be carving out these slices at a different angle.

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.480
They won't be parallel to my slices of time.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:18.120
To get a feel for the bizarre effect this can have,

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:23.130
imagine an alien here in a galaxy 10 billion light years

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:26.170
from Earth and way over there on Earth,

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:28.410
the guy at the gas station.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:29.770
Now.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:32.100
If the two are sitting still,

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:34.300
not moving in relation to one another,

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:37.060
their clocks tick off time at the same rate,

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:40.960
and so they share the same now slices,

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:43.760
which cut straight across the loaf.

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:48.890
But watch what happens if the alien hops on his bike

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:51.310
and rides directly away from Earth.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:54.540
Since motion slows the passage of time,

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.580
their clocks will no longer tick off time at the same rate.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.820
And if their clocks no longer agree,

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:05.850
their now slices will no longer agree either.

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:10.470
The alien's now slice cuts through the loaf differently.

00:25:10.940 --> 00:25:13.380
It's angled toward the past.

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:18.390
Since the alien is biking at a leisurely pace,

00:25:18.610 --> 00:25:22.990
his slice is angled to the past by only a minuscule amount.

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:25.980
But across such a vast distance,

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:29.760
that tiny angle results in a huge difference.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:37.540
time. So what the alien would find on his angled now slice, what he considers is happening right

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:44.190
now on Earth, no longer includes our friend at the gas station or even 40 years earlier when our

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:52.930
friend was a baby. Amazingly, the alien's now slice has swept back through 200 years of Earth

00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:58.340
history and now includes events that we consider part of the distant past, like

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:03.420
Beethoven finishing the Fifth Symphony.

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:05.990
Even at a relatively slow speed,

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:08.270
we can have actually tremendous disagreements

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:11.070
on our labeling of now, what happens at the same time,

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:14.220
if we're spread out far enough in space.

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:17.320
And if that's not strange enough,

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:20.790
the direction you move makes a difference too.

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:24.100
Watch what happens when the alien turns around

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:27.070
and bikes toward Earth.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:29.920
The aliens knew now

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.100
slice is angled toward the future.

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:37.200
And so, it includes events that won't happen on Earth

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:39.120
for 200 years.

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:43.090
Perhaps our friend's great-great-great-granddaughter

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:46.520
teleporting from Paris to New York.

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:51.580
Once we know that your now can be what I consider the past,

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:54.040
or your now can be what I consider the future,

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:58.010
and your now is every bit as valid as my now,

00:26:58.240 --> 00:26:59.560
then we learn that the past

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:00.740
must be real.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:02.640
The future must be real.

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:03.860
They could be your now.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:08.120
That means past, present, future, all equally real.

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:10.130
They all exist.

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:14.260
If you believe the laws of physics,

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:18.780
there's just as much reality to the future and the past

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:20.100
as there is to the present moment.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:25.530
The past is not gone, and the future isn't nonexistent.

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:27.890
Past, the future, and the present are all

00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:29.910
existing in exactly the same way.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:34.960
Just as we think of all of space as being out there,

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:39.460
we should think of all of time as being out there too.

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.200
Everything that has ever happened or will happen,

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:45.400
it all exists.

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:52.090
From Leonardo da Vinci laying the final brushstroke

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:56.120
on the Mona Lisa, to the signing

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:57.780
of the Declaration of Independence.

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:05.290
to your first day at school, to events

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:07.990
that, from our perspective, are yet to happen,

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:10.840
like the first humans landing on Mars.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:15.420
With this bold insight, Einstein shattered

00:28:15.420 --> 00:28:19.000
one of the most basic concepts of how we experience time.

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:23.080
The distinction between past, present, and future,

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:27.430
he once said, is only an illusion, however persistent.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:37.810
But if every moment in time already exists,

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.880
then how do we explain the very real feeling

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:47.680
that time, like this river, seems to endlessly rush forward?

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:53.310
Well, maybe we've been deceived and time does not flow.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:58.430
Perhaps the river of time is more like a frozen river.

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:04.350
with every moment forever locked in place.

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:10.280
The most vivid example about the way the world is

00:29:10.280 --> 00:29:12.240
has to do with this flow of time.

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:16.980
Physics does radical violence

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:20.380
to this everyday experience of time.

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:24.410
Our entire experience of time is constantly in the present,

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:27.550
and all we ever grasp is that instant moment.

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:29.980
There is nothing in the law...

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:36.360
of physics that picks out one now over any other now. And it's just from our subjective

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:42.520
viewpoints that it feels like things are changing. Just the way an entire movie exists on a reel

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:49.470
of celluloid, think of all moments of time as already existing, too. The difference is that in

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:56.470
the movies, a projector lights up or selects each frame as it goes by. But in the laws of physics,

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:59.890
there's no evidence of something like a projector.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.560
that selects one moment over another.

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:05.760
Our brains may create this impression,

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:10.440
but in reality, what we all experience as the flow of time

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:14.850
really may be nothing more than an illusion.

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:22.370
But if time, like this frozen river, does not flow

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.160
and all of time is out there,

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:29.960
is it possible to travel to the future or the past?

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:35.340
for 50 years in the future, like 24.

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:37.540
And if we could time travel,

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:39.960
would it be anything like what we imagine?

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:41.930
To catapult you through time

00:30:41.930 --> 00:30:44.090
into a world that is yet to be.

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:46.990
The time travelers.

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:50.210
Suppose something goes wrong with the time machine again.

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:51.310
Roll the switch jet.

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:54.310
Can we go anywhere we want at any time?

00:30:54.430 --> 00:30:56.580
We're going to attempt time travel.

00:30:56.880 --> 00:30:59.860
No one outside of Hollywood has made a working time.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:05.550
machine just yet. But surprisingly, time travel might be possible.

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:10.010
Now boarding, light 24 to black hole Cygnus X1.

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:15.650
One way to travel through time is to make use of a strange feature of gravity.

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:22.090
The familiar force that keeps our feet planted to the ground can have a profound impact on time.

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:29.830
Hi. See you later, sir. Right.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:31.230
Much later.

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:44.470
So how can gravity be used to make a time machine?

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:50.190
Well, Einstein's theory show that gravity, like motion,

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:51.390
can affect time.

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:56.140
It's as if gravity can pull on time, slowing its passage.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:04.840
And the stronger the gravitational pull, the more time slows.

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:09.500
Here on Earth, the effect is too small to notice,

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.310
but still very real.

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:17.470
Compared to someone living on the top floor of a skyscraper,

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:21.760
someone living on the bottom experiences time elapsing

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:26.120
a little slower because gravity is just a tiny bit stronger

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:27.400
closer to the ground.

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:32.820
But if you could travel to a black hole,

00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:36.800
the effect of gravity on time would be huge.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:41.590
Formed when large stars collapse in on themselves,

00:32:41.910 --> 00:32:44.690
black holes have immense gravitational pull,

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:48.760
millions and even billions of times stronger than the Earth's.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:55.380
If someone watched you travel close to a black hole,

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:59.300
they'd see time for you slow down dramatically.

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:11.130
You near that black hole will appear to your friend far away to be moving slowly, talking slowly, biologically, aging slowly.

00:33:11.410 --> 00:33:14.670
To them, years are passing, while for you it might be minutes.

00:33:14.910 --> 00:33:29.980
So depending on the black hole size and how close I get, if I spend an hour or two in orbit, something like 50 years will have passed back on Earth.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.160
I will have traveled to Earth's future.

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:35.570
Hello, sir.

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:36.130
Hi.

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:37.430
Long time no see.

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:39.550
Time travel becomes you.

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:40.030
Thank you.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:41.920
Kind of like a fountain of youth.

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:48.390
So, when I return, I'll find myself in the future.

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:51.330
Everyone else will have aged 50 years.

00:33:52.230 --> 00:33:52.750
But me?

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:55.280
I'll have aged only a couple of hours.

00:33:58.140 --> 00:33:59.960
Now, time travel to the future...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.260
is one thing, but what about time travel to the past?

00:34:05.590 --> 00:34:08.700
Well, that might be possible, too,

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:12.200
using something predicted by Einstein's equations

00:34:12.200 --> 00:34:13.500
known as a wormhole.

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:22.420
If wormholes exist, they would be kind of like shortcuts

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:25.230
through spacetime, tunnels that would link

00:34:25.230 --> 00:34:26.910
not just one place with another,

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.420
but also one moment with another.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:36.020
A wormhole would connect one part in space-time to another part in space-time,

00:34:36.140 --> 00:34:40.930
which is at an earlier time, like a sort of subway system through time.

00:34:43.610 --> 00:34:49.480
So let's say I wanted to go back in time and meet myself at the beginning of this program.

00:34:50.220 --> 00:34:55.130
If a wormhole connected here and there, all I'd need to do is step through.

00:35:06.200 --> 00:35:06.440
Thanks.

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:07.530
Good to be back.

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:12.720
Well, that would be kind of weird.

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:17.940
But the real problem with time travel to the past is that things would get pretty confusing

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:19.180
pretty quickly.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:24.600
I mean, imagine I were to change something about my past, like preventing my parents from

00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:24.880
meeting.

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:27.230
Would that mean I'd never be born?

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:29.970
If you do travel to the past, you...

00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:33.870
can't change things that we know are true about the past because they already happen.

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:37.810
So if you go back and kill who you thought was your grandpa,

00:35:37.950 --> 00:35:40.310
that must have been some other guy you thought was your grandfather,

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:43.860
and everything must somehow become beautifully self-consistent,

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:45.660
even if it's in a twisted way.

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:49.920
And if you can travel to the past,

00:35:50.500 --> 00:35:53.980
why haven't we been overrun by tourists from the future?

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:56.080
I mean, think about it.

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:59.970
We haven't seen any intrepid time travelers popping into...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:03.800
and out of our world, at least most people don't think we have.

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:05.860
So it's probably safe to assume

00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:09.000
that time travel to the past just is impossible,

00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:11.320
at least not yet.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:14.950
But since the math hasn't yet ruled it out,

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:18.510
we can't dismiss time travel to the past entirely.

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:22.420
So it's not at all clear that it could ever

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:26.300
be a practical reality, but at least in principle,

00:36:26.620 --> 00:36:28.240
it doesn't seem to be forbidden.

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:29.980
My guess is that it's impossible.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:35.560
but it's striking that we still haven't been able to rigorously prove that.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:40.780
While it seems likely that traveling to the past is out of reach,

00:36:41.060 --> 00:36:44.540
what about the fact, so common to our everyday experience,

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:50.780
that time itself seems to move in only one direction, toward the future?

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:55.450
Call this the arrow of time.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:36:59.980
The arrow of time is probably the most blatant...

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.000
fact about the universe we live in that we don't completely understand. Why we

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:12.370
live in a universe that has a directionality to time is a mystery. This

00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:17.150
is not true of space. In space I can go from New York to Chicago and then I can

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:21.960
change my mind and go from Chicago to New York. So there is a one-way aspect to

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:26.150
time that we don't understand at a fundamental level. Why doesn't it go

00:37:26.150 --> 00:37:29.040
backwards? What does it even mean that

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.100
Time goes forward from the past into the future.

00:37:34.030 --> 00:37:37.810
So what can we say about where the arrow of time comes from?

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:41.540
Why do we only see events unfold in one direction?

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:44.510
Why don't we ever see them happen in reverse order?

00:37:45.070 --> 00:37:47.320
Well, it must be the laws of physics.

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:50.620
I mean, surely they don't allow something like this to happen.

00:37:57.020 --> 00:37:58.810
Well, actually, they do.

00:37:59.500 --> 00:37:59.960
The laws...

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:05.520
of physics are the mathematical equations we use to describe everything from the behavior

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:11.660
of atoms to the swirl of galaxies. They've been devised and confirmed through centuries

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:18.240
of observation and experiment. But surprisingly, there's nothing in the laws of physics that

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:24.980
says events have to unfold through the familiar sequence we call forward in time. According

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:29.640
to these equations, events could just as well unfold in reverse order.

00:38:31.310 --> 00:38:35.230
Most of the equations we use to describe what we see in the universe around us

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:37.760
don't have an arrow of time attached to them.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:43.310
They're equations that work equally well, moving forward in time, moving backwards in time.

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:47.730
There's this contradiction between the physics, which seems fundamentally reversible,

00:38:48.280 --> 00:38:51.700
and so much of our life that seems irreversible.

00:38:56.590 --> 00:38:59.410
Though it flies in the face of everyday experience,

00:38:59.410 --> 00:38:59.970
Thank you.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:04.660
The laws of physics actually say bizarre things like these are possible.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:06.940
But how could this be?

00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:12.840
Well, the answer is not as far-fetched as you might think.

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:15.580
Here's why.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:19.560
We all know what will happen if I drop this glass of wine.

00:39:29.540 --> 00:39:29.900
Now...

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:36.310
The idea that this mess could somehow reverse itself and form back into a solid glass filled

00:39:36.310 --> 00:39:37.770
with wine seems absurd.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:42.030
But according to the laws of physics, this can happen.

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:45.490
All I need to do is reverse the velocities of everything.

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:53.440
Every piece of glass, every drop of wine, every molecule and atom in the liquid glass,

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:57.180
table and air, just reverse all their velocities.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:00.840
And voila!

00:40:09.620 --> 00:40:16.180
So, if the laws of physics don't care about whether glasses shatter or unshatter, why don't

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:17.960
we ever see them unshatter?

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:21.220
How can we square the laws of physics with our everyday experience?

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:25.050
Something must be missing in our understanding, but what?

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:27.410
What's responsible for the arrow of time?

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:42.890
Like many good mysteries, this one leads us to a graveyard in our search for clues.

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:51.060
In Vienna, near the final resting places of Beethoven, Brahms, Schubert, and Strauss,

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:56.340
is 19th century Austrian physicist Ludwig Boltzmann's tombstone.

00:40:57.450 --> 00:40:59.970
Etched on top is an elegant equation.

00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:08.590
It's the mathematical formulation of a powerful concept known as entropy.

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:17.340
Entropy is a measure of something that we're all familiar with, disorder or randomness.

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:24.790
And it's an important idea because there's a tendency of everything in the universe to move from order to disorder.

00:41:25.280 --> 00:41:26.820
Here's a way to get a feel for the idea.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.970
Take my book, All 560...

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:37.340
It's very ordered, with the first page, followed by the second, followed by the third, and so on.

00:41:37.870 --> 00:41:41.870
But now, let's tear the pages out and let entropy go to work.

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:53.750
As you can see, the pages become very disordered, and the reason is simple.

00:41:54.820 --> 00:41:59.970
There is only one way for them to land in order, but a huge difference.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:06.280
number of ways for them to land out of order. And so it's much more likely that they'll land

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:14.180
in a total mess. And this is what we experience in our daily lives. Things move from order to

00:42:14.260 --> 00:42:19.930
disorder. In this case, from a neat ordered book to pages that are randomly scattered.

00:42:20.920 --> 00:42:27.760
Everywhere we look, we see examples of entropy or disorder increasing with the passage of time.

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.000
An egg breaks and splatters.

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:36.940
Ice cubes lose their orderly shape as they melt into water.

00:42:38.390 --> 00:42:41.480
Fillowing smoke becomes increasingly disordered.

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:45.140
Ordered states become disordered states.

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:47.940
And that appears to be, perhaps,

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:49.940
the direction of an arrow of time.

00:42:50.870 --> 00:42:53.520
We see sort of degrees of messiness,

00:42:53.620 --> 00:42:54.880
a measure of disorder,

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:57.820
tend to increase in one direction of time.

00:42:58.240 --> 00:42:59.980
And so that, for Boltzmann,

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:02.180
begins to create an arc of time.

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:06.050
So maybe this is the answer.

00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:10.250
Maybe the arrow of time comes from the tendency of nature

00:43:10.250 --> 00:43:12.980
to evolve toward ever greater disorder.

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:17.340
This sure seems like progress,

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:21.080
but there's just one small problem with this reasoning.

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:24.260
Because the laws of physics don't distinguish

00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:26.020
between the future and the past,

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:29.000
entropy should increase not only toward the future,

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:29.970
but also toward the future.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:32.690
toward the past, and that makes no sense.

00:43:33.980 --> 00:43:36.120
That's like saying that entropy should increase

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:37.660
in either direction that we look.

00:43:39.170 --> 00:43:41.430
We could look backwards in time, and it should increase.

00:43:41.690 --> 00:43:43.370
We could look forwards in time, and it should increase.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:47.020
That would mean the pages of my book in the past

00:43:47.020 --> 00:43:49.860
would be disordered, and then come together

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:52.680
to form the neat ordered book in my hands.

00:43:54.170 --> 00:43:57.590
And when's the last time you saw something like that happen?

00:43:58.610 --> 00:43:59.930
How could our everyday experience

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.460
be so at odds with the laws of physics?

00:44:03.370 --> 00:44:05.790
There must be a piece of the puzzle that's missing.

00:44:07.590 --> 00:44:10.490
If we're sure the past had to be more ordered

00:44:10.490 --> 00:44:12.700
and that everything tends toward disorder

00:44:13.160 --> 00:44:15.420
as the equations of entropy tell us,

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:19.380
is there something else besides the laws of physics

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:21.160
that might explain this?

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:26.030
Well, think of hitting a baseball.

00:44:27.890 --> 00:44:29.830
The laws of physics can help you predict

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:30.680
where it will land.

00:44:32.600 --> 00:44:36.160
But those laws are not the only things you need.

00:44:37.030 --> 00:44:39.160
Run the film backward, and you can

00:44:39.160 --> 00:44:41.560
see that you also need the initial conditions,

00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:44.350
like how hard the ball was hit.

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:48.900
Similarly, if the laws of physics can't give us

00:44:48.900 --> 00:44:50.540
an explanation for the hour of time,

00:44:50.740 --> 00:44:53.460
maybe we need to look further to the initial conditions

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:54.610
of the universe.

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:57.700
That brings our attention back to the Big Bang.

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:05.060
If the history of the universe were like a movie, and you ran it backwards,

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:09.990
you'd see an increase in order the further back in time you go.

00:45:11.620 --> 00:45:16.700
Gradually, today's universe, with billions of galaxies clumped here and there,

00:45:17.070 --> 00:45:22.320
would turn back into clouds of gas and dust as everything contracts.

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:27.240
These clouds of gas and dust move closer and closer to each other,

00:45:27.280 --> 00:45:29.580
so that if you get far enough into the past,

00:45:29.580 --> 00:45:29.860
the

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:32.800
are squeezed into a smaller and smaller volume.

00:45:34.180 --> 00:45:37.200
We've now come to the place where the buck finally stops.

00:45:37.880 --> 00:45:41.940
If this represents all of space at each moment of time,

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:45.140
then we can see there simply isn't any more space

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:48.770
in time before this single moment.

00:45:49.600 --> 00:45:53.080
So the ultimate source of order, of low entropy,

00:45:53.440 --> 00:45:57.350
must be the very beginning of the universe, the Big Bang.

00:45:59.540 --> 00:45:59.980
Big Bang.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:01.780
is a highly ordered state.

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:05.220
It's probably the most ordered

00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:07.400
event in all of physics.

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:12.560
And so everything that has come

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:14.580
after that has been an increase

00:46:14.580 --> 00:46:18.380
in disorder. What the Big Bang gives us

00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:20.160
is a reason why the universe might look

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:22.190
different when we look backwards in time

00:46:22.190 --> 00:46:24.590
versus forward. Moreover,

00:46:24.850 --> 00:46:27.010
when we go back to early times, the universe should have looked

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:29.010
not just different from today, but highly

00:46:29.010 --> 00:46:29.450
ordered.

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:32.420
Why was the entropy low? We don't know.

00:46:32.980 --> 00:46:37.500
But at least we know that there was a point that the universe began in when the entropy was low.

00:46:38.420 --> 00:46:44.080
So our best understanding is that the Big Bang is what set the hour of time on its path.

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:49.470
You can picture this as something like a wind-up clock.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:55.330
Just as the stored energy of a tightly wound clock is released as it unwinds,

00:46:55.980 --> 00:46:59.060
the universe has been unwinding since the Big Bang.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:01.500
becoming ever more disordered.

00:47:02.250 --> 00:47:05.810
Our universe started out in a very unusually orderly state.

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:12.870
That's ultimately responsible for the fact that time seems to have a direction.

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:18.560
We don't yet know why our universe began in a highly ordered state,

00:47:19.060 --> 00:47:23.160
but the fact that it did means that every time a glass shatters,

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:28.410
it's actually carrying forward something set in motion billions of years ago.

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:29.970
The glass breaks...

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:38.010
but doesn't unbreak because it's following the natural drive from order to disorder that began with the Big Bang.

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:42.390
We only ever move from the past to the future.

00:47:42.830 --> 00:47:47.630
And everything we see around us, all the changes from the formation of stars to our lives,

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:53.970
is all little epiphenomena, surfers riding the wave of increasing disorganization in the universe

00:47:53.970 --> 00:47:56.610
that defines the difference between the past and the future.

00:47:57.340 --> 00:47:59.780
So, the Big Bang have stamped

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:05.620
the arrow of time on our universe and everything that's happened since may simply be the drive

00:48:05.620 --> 00:48:12.780
toward ever greater disorder that began with that event 13.7 billion years ago.

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:20.100
But if time had a beginning and disorder is always increasing, does that mean that time

00:48:20.100 --> 00:48:21.000
will have an end?

00:48:21.620 --> 00:48:25.730
What will the universe be like in the far, far future?

00:48:28.420 --> 00:48:29.980
Recent discoveries are shedding...

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.380
new light on this question.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.090
The explosive force of the Big Bang

00:48:39.240 --> 00:48:41.260
sent space hurtling outward,

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:44.230
and as a result, the universe is still

00:48:44.230 --> 00:48:48.460
expanding today. Until recently,

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:50.520
most people thought that expansion

00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:53.730
must be slowing down. That is,

00:48:53.870 --> 00:48:55.670
we thought of space filled with

00:48:55.670 --> 00:48:57.790
galaxies as kind of like a car

00:48:57.790 --> 00:48:58.970
traveling down a highway.

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:10.460
If the driver takes his foot off the gas, the car gradually slows down.

00:49:11.390 --> 00:49:16.490
Similarly, we thought the universe was expanding, but at a slower and slower rate.

00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:22.420
Surprisingly, astronomers found the expansion of the universe is not slowing down.

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:24.120
It's accelerating.

00:49:25.510 --> 00:49:29.970
It's as if someone's not taking their foot off the gas pedal, but stepping on it.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.310
on it, causing a turbo booster to kick in, and that's making the expansion of the universe

00:49:36.310 --> 00:49:38.550
speed up more and more.

00:49:40.940 --> 00:49:44.000
Our expansion will keep accelerating in the future, not slow down.

00:49:44.600 --> 00:49:47.080
It goes against everything we had kind of gotten used to thinking about.

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:51.890
This has some very strange implications for the future.

00:49:53.030 --> 00:49:59.940
Because the expansion of our universe is accelerating, the far future, after a hundred billion

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:08.070
years or so, all of the other distant galaxies will have hurtled out of sight from us. It will

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:18.680
appear as if our galaxy were in the middle of nothing. A surprising outcome is that our

00:50:18.680 --> 00:50:26.460
descendants will be at a terrible loss. Light from distant galaxies has to travel so far to reach us

00:50:26.460 --> 00:50:29.970
that when we look out at them, we're out of sight.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:31.610
actually looking back in time.

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:37.080
So in the far future, when those distant galaxies

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:39.350
are no longer visible, astronomers

00:50:39.350 --> 00:50:43.290
will find that the past, in cosmic terms, is out of reach.

00:50:43.450 --> 00:50:51.060
And as for the end of time, one theory

00:50:51.060 --> 00:50:55.100
suggests that eventually, black holes will dominate the cosmos.

00:50:58.570 --> 00:50:59.970
Then they too will.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:06.080
evaporate, leaving nothing but random particles drifting through space.

00:51:08.180 --> 00:51:13.840
In a far distant future where everything has decayed and everything's just sort of smoothed

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:18.690
out, there's no change. And without change, we don't really have a clear notion of the

00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:19.310
passage of time.

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:27.440
If you don't have events happening, then it's hard to see how you would even imagine that

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:28.330
there was time.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:29.460
You can't even tell.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.660
which direction of time is forward and which is backward.

00:51:33.370 --> 00:51:37.180
In a very real sense, time itself will one day lose its meaning.

00:51:44.940 --> 00:51:48.200
About 350 years ago, Isaac Newton,

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:51.540
who was one of the first to think about time scientifically,

00:51:52.220 --> 00:51:55.140
wrote that he did not need to define time

00:51:55.160 --> 00:51:58.000
because it is something well-known to all.

00:51:59.150 --> 00:51:59.970
But in tr-

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.240
Trying to square our experience of time with the true nature of time,

00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:10.210
we've been forced to challenge some of our most deeply held beliefs.

00:52:13.050 --> 00:52:17.140
We now know that in every event that goes from order to disorder,

00:52:18.330 --> 00:52:23.630
there's a link to the Big Bang itself giving us the arrow of time.

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:29.970
The common sense notion that one true time governs the universe has given...

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:37.420
way to a picture in which time is different for each and every one of us and the flow of time which

00:52:37.510 --> 00:52:44.600
seems to us as real as the flow of a river may be nothing more than an illusion past present and

00:52:44.600 --> 00:52:52.880
future may all exist on equal footing our everyday experience of time will always exert

00:52:52.880 --> 00:52:59.970
a powerful influence we will continue to imagine that time is universal that the past

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:06.060
is gone that the future is yet to be but because of our scientific discoveries we

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:11.440
can also look beyond experience and recognize that we are part of a far

00:53:11.440 --> 00:53:29.380
richer and far stranger reality major funding for NOVA is provided by David

